/// A host capture stall suspends input for a stale view (plan 11.3); it is not
/// a lost lease. Once control resumes on the same lease, copies still cross
/// both ways on the same clipboard lane and its one X11 child (fr-kz3k).
#[test]
#[ignore = "explicit isolated user/mount/network namespace; synthetic ingress; two Xvfb displays; real input agent and clipboard child"]
fn a_capture_stall_keeps_the_clipboard_crossing_both_ways() {
    let mut s = Controlled::start_with(true, true);
    let mut host = Peer::start(&s.host.display);
    let mut viewer = Peer::start(&s.viewer.display);
    let before = unique("before the stall");
    viewer.copy(&before);
    if let Err(last) = pasted(&mut host, &before, Duration::from_secs(20)) {
        panic!(
            "lane never worked (last {last:?}); host: {}",
            s.daemon.dump()
        );
    }
    let painter = Painter::start(&s.host.display);
    thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(1500));
    stall_capture(400);
    assert!(
        s.pointer_follows((300, 250), Duration::from_secs(20)),
        "control did not resume after the stall: {}",
        s.daemon.dump()
    );
    let to_host = unique("viewer→host after the stall");
    viewer.copy(&to_host);
    let host_got = pasted(&mut host, &to_host, Duration::from_secs(20));
    let to_viewer = unique("host→viewer after the stall");
    host.copy(&to_viewer);
    let viewer_got = pasted(&mut viewer, &to_viewer, Duration::from_secs(20));
    let children = clipboard_children();
    drop(painter);
    s.close_viewer();
    let output = wait_for(s.client, Duration::from_secs(30));
    let report = completion(&output, &s.daemon.dump());
    println!("fr after a capture stall with the clipboard: {report}");
    assert!(
        host_got.is_ok(),
        "host never pasted the post-stall copy (last {host_got:?}); {report}"
    );
    assert!(
        viewer_got.is_ok(),
        "viewer never pasted the post-stall copy (last {viewer_got:?}); {report}"
    );
    assert_eq!(children, 1, "one X11 owner for the one lane");
    assert_eq!(report["outcome"], "stopped", "{report}");
    assert_eq!(report["attempts"], 1, "{report}");
    assert_eq!(report["clipboard_active"], true, "{report}");
    assert_eq!(report["clipboard_absence"], serde_json::Value::Null);
    assert!(
        report["last_attempt_media"]["recovered_streams"]
            .as_u64()
            .is_some_and(|n| n >= 1),
        "the stall did not exercise a recovery: {report}"
    );
    assert!(
        report["input_suspensions"].as_u64().is_some_and(|n| n >= 1),
        "the stall did not suspend input, so it proves nothing: {report}"
    );
    drop((host, viewer));
    s.daemon.finish();
}

2026-11-08: one way a busy host ends a healthy connection, shown by a test. Not yet tied to any live run.

fr-transport closes the connection when a retained reliable record is still unacknowledged at its delivery deadline, max(send-by, admission + delivery_allowance_us), which is 250 ms on a fast path. QuicRecords::check judges that deadline against the local clock at the sender's next call, before the sender has read the socket. If the sender's own task does not run for longer than the allowance, its first call after waking ends the connection with Expired, even when the peer acknowledged in time and the ACK is sitting in the socket buffer.

Test (fr-transport/tests/native_quic.rs, not committed, text below). The server sends one record. The client receives it and ACKs within 60 ms. The server then sleeps until admission + 350 ms without driving, and drives once. On main 720ec971, built and run on trj: 3 of 3 runs fail. drive returns Err(Expired) and closes the connection. The other 22 native_quic tests pass.

Where the judgement runs before a read:
- tick, send and receive call check() synchronously and cannot read. tick's doc says so on purpose ("without reading another stream").
- drive's wrapper checks first. drive_turn checks again, poll_io_at_boundary refuses at `at >= until` before polling, and the tail check (quic.rs ~996) runs before the loop that retires the epochs the read just acknowledged (~1005).
- The host's controlled loop (session_startup/running.rs drive_inner) runs maintain's synchronous calls first: renewal.receive, renewal.service, then input. Only after those does it drive. So after a late wake, the first judgement is one that cannot read.
- The check covers every retained record on the connection. The error lands on whichever caller checks first, not on the record's own service.

C9's host ending (fr-live-invalid-routes-45mn), ControlRenewal(Transport(Expired)), has this shape. But a client that stopped sending ACKs for 250 ms produces the same code. C9 had no host memory stall. ts2 CPU pressure was never sampled (the sampler logs memory and io only), so a host scheduling stall there is unknown, not excluded.

Why I did not change it: it is a contract change. tick is documented to enforce retained-record deadlines without reading, and bulk_epochs relies on that. Its test closes a peer that never ACKs through tick alone, within 1.9 s. Options:
(A) Absorb before judging on the drive path. When a deadline is due, read once with a short wait, then judge. Retire acknowledged epochs before the tail check, and have the host loops drive before their synchronous services. This keeps tick's contract but only helps loops that read first. I wrote the transport half (about 100 lines) and did not build it. It is not in the repo.
(B) Judge each deadline against the start of the sender's last completed socket read, with an absolute fallback so a sender that never reads still closes in bounded time. Covers every caller. Changes tick's semantics, and probably bulk_epochs' bound.
(C) Keep it fail-closed and accept that a host stall longer than the allowance ends the session. Then the 250 ms floor is the knob.
This is the owner's call. The test below fails on main as written. For (A) or (B), it is the positive test, and a_peer_that_stops_acknowledging_still_closes_the_connection_in_bounded_time plus bulk_epochs are the negatives that must still pass.

/// A late wake-up of the sender is not a late peer. The peer read this record
/// and acknowledged it well inside its allowance; the sender only ran again
/// after the allowance, with that acknowledgement already in its socket.
#[test]
fn a_sender_that_wakes_late_keeps_a_record_its_peer_acknowledged_in_time() {
    run_test!(cx, {
        let mut p = pair(&cx, Policy::default()).await;
        let route = Route::Stream(p.host_routes[1]);
        measure_path(&cx, &mut p, route).await;
        let packet = recovery_packet(2, 100);
        let admitted = clock(&cx);
        p.server.send(&cx, route, &packet, admitted + 2_000, || true).unwrap();
        // Transmit it once, without waiting for any reply.
        p.server.drive(&cx, Duration::ZERO, || true).await.unwrap();
        // The peer reads and acknowledges it, well inside the allowance.
        let mut received = 0;
        while clock(&cx) < admitted + 60_000 {
            p.client.drive(&cx, Duration::from_millis(1), || true).await.unwrap();
            p.client
                .receive(&cx, || true, |_, b| {
                    assert_eq!(b, packet);
                    received += 1;
                    Ok(Disposition::Consumed)
                })
                .unwrap();
        }
        assert_eq!(received, 1);
        assert_eq!(p.server.usage().retained_send_records, 1);
        // The sender is not scheduled again until past its allowance.
        let late = MIN_DELIVERY_ALLOWANCE_US + 100_000;
        asupersync::time::sleep(cx.now(), Duration::from_micros(late)).await;
        assert!(clock(&cx) >= admitted + late);
        let result = p.server.drive(&cx, Duration::from_millis(1), || true).await;
        assert_eq!(result, Ok(()), "the sender's own late turn was judged as a late peer");
        assert!(!p.server.is_closed());
        assert_eq!(p.server.usage().retained_send_records, 0);
    });
}

