Interleaved live A/B, swapping only the worker (load 20-42):
- old: 19.2-19.9 pictures/s (3 sessions);
- new: 20.2-21.2 (6 sessions);
- p95 at most 73-76 ms old, 65-77 ms new.
After the change, the main thread spends 0% in tccd/WindowServer calls, 71% in the encode, 18% waiting for frd and 11% waiting for a frame.

What is left at 5K, in addition to the (a)-(d) levers in my earlier note:
(e) frd's pacing settles the capture interval at about 7/6 to 4/3 of the measured source work: it probes faster only when work <= 3/4 of the interval for 2 s, and doubles the interval after 100 ms of work >= interval. With one capture in flight nothing can queue at the source, so the worker sits idle about 18% of the time (~8.5 ms per picture) waiting for the next request. That headroom rule is the contributor's design; relaxing it for a one-in-flight source is a policy decision. My estimate is ~24-25 pictures/s from the same encode, not measured.
(f) One slow capture doubles the interval, and recovery is 1/8 per 2 s, so a single load spike can halve the rate for several seconds. That probably accounts for the 100-160 ms tail gaps under load (not isolated).

